Sure! One way to extend the lifetime of a variable in Rust is by creating a reference to it. For example, consider the following code:

```rust
fn main() {
    let my_string = String::from(hello);
    let my_ref = &my_string; // creates a reference to my_string
    // my_string will live until the end of this block because my_ref is borrowing it
    println!({}, my_ref);
}
```

In this code, `my_string` is a String variable that is created in the first line. Then, in the second line, a reference to `my_string` is created and assigned to `my_ref`. Because `my_ref` is borrowing `my_string`, the Rust compiler knows that `my_string` must live at least as long as `my_ref`. Therefore, `my_string` will not be dropped until the end of the block, which is after the `println!()` statement.

By creating a reference to `my_string`, we have effectively extended its lifetime beyond the end of the line where it was declared. This is just one example of how you can extend the lifetime of a variable in Rust.

There are a few ways accidental name conflicts can happen in C++:

Using a namespace and accidentally naming something the same as something in that namespace. For example:
using namespace std;
int vector = 5; // Oops, overrides std::vector!

Accidentally naming a local variable the same as a global variable or function. For example:
int foo = 5;

void bar() {
    int foo = 10; // Whoops, shadows the outer foo!
}

Accidentally naming a local variable the same as a type name. For example:
struct Foo { ... };

void bar() {
    int Foo = 5; // Ah crap, hides the type!
}

These kinds of conflicts can be hard to spot, especially in large codebases or when using long, descriptive names that increase the chance of a collision. The solution is to simply be aware of your naming and the scopes you're in, and use tools like IDEs that can warn you about potential name conflicts.

I would assume rst being neither Remote Switching Terminal, nor Resolved Sexual Tension. Therefore, in Rust, a URL is a standard string, which can be parsed to the following components via the url create:

use url::{Url, Host, Position};
let issue_list_url = Url::parse(
    https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues?labels=E-easy&state=open
)?;

assert!(issue_list_url.scheme() == https);
assert!(issue_list_url.username() == );
assert!(issue_list_url.password() == None);
assert!(issue_list_url.host_str() == Some(github.com));
assert!(issue_list_url.host() == Some(Host::Domain(github.com)));
assert!(issue_list_url.port() == None);
assert!(issue_list_url.path() == /rust-lang/rust/issues);
assert!(issue_list_url.path_segments().map(|c| c.collect::<Vec<_>>()) ==
        Some(vec![rust-lang, rust, issues]));
assert!(issue_list_url.query() == Some(labels=E-easy&state=open));
assert!(&issue_list_url[Position::BeforePath..] == /rust-lang/rust/issues?labels=E-easy&state=open);
assert!(issue_list_url.fragment() == None);
assert!(!issue_list_url.cannot_be_a_base());

